[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read for what they show, its reports hold three problems.
Each now has a ticket of its own.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued fix is the parser fix. It closes the parser ticket only; the hint and one-day reports keep coming
until their own fixes ship.
</Callout>

## How each report was placed

1. **One id, one report.** The ticket export comes in pages that overlap, so some reports come twice. Each id
   counts once.
2. **By tenant, as the desk counts.** `causeOf` in `causes.ts` looks at the tenant's locale, their booking
   form and the channel the report came in by: a day-first tenant's report through booking-v3's form is
   `hint`, any other way in is `parser`. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and is not used.
3. **One-day moves have their own row and ticket.** The desk read some reports as a date moved by one day
   (`desk-notes.json`). A month/day swap does not move a date by one day, so they are a problem of their own.
   As the desk counts them, they also stay counted under `parser`, where they are filed.

## The problems

### `parser`: every date is read month first

Ticket: [#1](https://github.com/kulfix/mega-what-it-shows-2/issues/1).
The shared parser reads dates month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so any date whose
day is 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped: a booking for 7 April lands on 4 July. This is
what #4471 describes, and its queued fix is the fix for this problem. Most of these reports show the swap
in their own dates; a few only say the day was wrong, and land here by their tenant and channel.

### `hint`: booking-v3 asks for the month first

Ticket: [#2](https://github.com/kulfix/mega-what-it-shows-2/issues/2).
The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, for tenants who write the day first.
People type what the hint asks; the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The result looks the same
as `parser`, but the parser fix does not touch it: the hint has to follow the tenant's locale. Only
reports sent through the form of a booking-v3 tenant land here.

### `one-day`: the date moves by one day

Ticket: [#3](https://github.com/kulfix/mega-what-it-shows-2/issues/3).

> Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting. We held it twice.

These reports describe a date that is one day off, or an invite that arrived before its booking was
made, never a swapped month. The cause is not known yet. Only the Lisbon/Toronto report points at time
zones; a date stored as a moment in time and shown in another zone would do this, but that is a guess
until someone reproduces it. It needs its own reproduction and its own fix.

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a report feels, not what broke. Different bugs share "wrong day".
- Before calling a merged ticket known, read what its own table shows: here, a row marked "no ticket of
  its own" and a footnote.
- Notes the desk already wrote (here: the one-day moves) are evidence. Read them before merging.

